  "post": {
    "caption": "Peggy Guggenheim wore mismatched earrings. On October 20, 1942, she opened her gallery Art of This Century in New York. She wore one earring by Yves Tanguy and one by Alexander Calder, and said it showed her impartiality between Surrealist and Abstract art. Tanguy's was a tiny oil landscape painted on shell, and Calder's was a wire mobile in miniature. Calder first made jewelry for his sister's dolls, and went on to make roughly 1,800 pieces. He used only pliers and a hammer, with no welds. Both pairs are kept in the Peggy Guggenheim Collection in Venice.",
